(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-4-renal-physiology-1-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Renal I: Fluid Regulation & GFR — Quiz Set 2 (30 Questions)","category":"Physiology","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"According to the lecture's innervation summary table, which nerve originates from S2–S4 and drives detrusor contraction?","choices":["Genitofemoral nerve","Pudendal nerve","Pelvic nerve","Hypogastric nerve"],"answer":2,"exp":"The pelvic nerve, originating from S2–S4, drives detrusor contraction. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Per the lecture's innervation summary table, the hypogastric nerve (T11–L2/L1–L2) functions primarily in:","choices":["Sensory stretch detection only","Detrusor contraction","Storage","External sphincter control"],"answer":2,"exp":"The hypogastric nerve's listed function is storage. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which nerve, per the innervation summary table, controls the external sphincter and originates from S2–S4?","choices":["Pudendal nerve","Hypogastric nerve","Vagus nerve","Pelvic nerve"],"answer":0,"exp":"The pudendal nerve, originating from S2–S4, controls the external sphincter. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Summarized as a single pathway, the micturition reflex proceeds: Bladder Stretch → Pelvic Afferents (S2–S4) → Sacral Micturition Center → Parasympathetic Efferents → which final step?","choices":["Sympathetic efferent activation of the hypogastric nerve","External sphincter contraction only, with no detrusor involvement","Detrusor contraction and internal sphincter relaxation, leading to urination","Detrusor relaxation and internal sphincter contraction, preventing urination"],"answer":2,"exp":"The summarized pathway ends in detrusor contraction plus internal sphincter relaxation, producing urination. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"How does an individual voluntarily initiate urination even before the bladder reaches its natural reflex threshold?","choices":["By voluntarily contracting abdominal muscles to raise bladder pressure and stretch the bladder neck/posterior urethra, exciting the reflex, while relaxing the external sphincter","By completely and permanently inhibiting all afferent and efferent signaling carried through the pelvic nerve pathway to the bladder wall.","By voluntarily and forcefully constricting both ureters near the bladder wall to push urine in ahead of the natural filling schedule.","There is no voluntary mechanism whatsoever that allows a person to initiate urination before the natural reflex threshold is reached."],"answer":0,"exp":"Voluntarily contracting the abdominal muscles increases bladder pressure, forcing urine into the bladder neck/posterior urethra, which stretches and stimulates stretch receptors to excite the micturition reflex, while the external sphincter is simultaneously relaxed. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Once the micturition reflex becomes powerful enough, it triggers a second reflex that travels through which nerve to inhibit the external sphincter?","choices":["Genitofemoral nerve","Hypogastric nerve","Pelvic nerve","Pudendal nerve"],"answer":3,"exp":"A sufficiently powerful micturition reflex triggers a second reflex that passes through the pudendal nerves to inhibit the external sphincter. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Under normal conditions, how do higher brain centers typically influence the spinal micturition reflex?","choices":["They have no influence at all on the reflex, which operates as a purely isolated, self-contained spinal circuit.","They keep the reflex partially inhibited except when urination is desired, and can prevent it via tonic contraction of the external sphincter","They permanently and completely abolish the underlying spinal micturition reflex once puberty has been reached.","They only ever facilitate and progressively strengthen the reflex, and are never capable of inhibiting it."],"answer":1,"exp":"Higher centers normally keep the micturition reflex partially inhibited except when urination is desired, and can prevent micturition — even if the reflex occurs — through tonic contraction of the external sphincter. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Why is the micturition reflex described as 'self-regenerative'?","choices":["Because each new contraction cycle is carried out through an entirely different, previously unused nerve pathway to the bladder wall each time.","Because once the first contraction is triggered, the entire cycle continues entirely on its own without requiring any further neural input whatsoever.","Because an initial contraction further stimulates stretch receptors, increasing sensory impulses that increase reflex contractions, repeating until the bladder achieves a strong contraction","Because the bladder wall's detrusor smooth muscle tissue is physically regenerated and structurally repaired after each complete voiding event."],"answer":2,"exp":"The reflex is self-regenerative because an initial contraction activates stretch receptors, further increasing sensory impulses from the bladder/posterior urethra, which further increases reflex contractions — a cycle that repeats until the bladder reaches a strong degree of contraction. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A single complete micturition reflex cycle includes which sequence of pressure changes?","choices":["A period of initially sustained pressure, then a slow gradual decline, then one final pressure spike.","An immediate return to basal tone with no rise in pressure or sustained phase at all.","A progressive and rapid increase of pressure, a period of sustained pressure, then a return to basal tone","A single instantaneous pressure spike occurring with absolutely no other distinct phases."],"answer":2,"exp":"A single complete cycle includes a progressive and rapid increase of pressure, a period of sustained pressure, and then a return of pressure to the basal tone of the bladder. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"If a micturition reflex cycle occurs but fails to empty the bladder, what happens next according to the lecture?","choices":["The nervous elements controlling it remain inhibited permanently","The bladder immediately empties via a backup sympathetic reflex","No further reflexes can occur until the next micturition event days later","The nervous elements remain inhibited for a period before the cycle can occur again"],"answer":3,"exp":"Once a micturition reflex has occurred but not succeeded in emptying the bladder, the nervous elements controlling it remain inhibited for a period of time before another reflex occurs. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Micturition is fundamentally an autonomic spinal cord reflex, but according to the lecture, what normally exerts 'final control' over it?","choices":["The sacral micturition center alone, with no higher input","Higher brain centers","The pelvic floor muscles exclusively","The renal autoregulatory system"],"answer":1,"exp":"While micturition is basically an autonomic spinal cord reflex, higher centers normally exert the final control over it. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"In the summarized micturition pathway, which segment of the spinal cord houses the sacral micturition center that pelvic afferents synapse onto?","choices":["C1–C4","S2–S4","T11–L2","L1–L2 only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Pelvic afferents from S2–S4 synapse onto the sacral micturition center in that same S2–S4 region of the spinal cord. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"During voluntary urination, what happens to the external sphincter at the same time the abdominal muscles contract to raise bladder pressure?","choices":["It has no involvement in voluntary urination","It contracts further and more tightly to actively prevent leakage","It is simultaneously relaxed to allow urine to pass","It is denervated during voluntary micturition"],"answer":2,"exp":"While abdominal muscle contraction raises bladder pressure and excites the micturition reflex via stretch, the external sphincter is simultaneously relaxed so urine can pass. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"In the general clearance equation C = (U × V) / P, what does 'U' represent?","choices":["Ultrafiltration coefficient","Plasma concentration of the substance","Urine flow rate","Urine concentration of the substance"],"answer":3,"exp":"In the clearance equation, U is the urine concentration of the substance being measured. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which substance is described as being freely filtered and used as a classic marker for measuring GFR via clearance?","choices":["Inulin","Albumin","Hemoglobin","Fibrinogen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Inulin clearance is listed as a way to calculate GFR because inulin is freely filtered. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Creatinine, used to estimate GFR via clearance, is produced by which tissue?","choices":["Skeletal muscle","Adipose tissue","Hepatic tissue","The pancreas"],"answer":0,"exp":"Creatinine is produced by skeletal muscle, is freely filtered, and is only minimally secreted, making its clearance a practical (if slightly overestimating) proxy for true GFR. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Compared to typical capillary membranes elsewhere in the body, the glomerular capillary membrane's Kf is approximately:","choices":["About 4 times greater","About the same as typical capillaries","About 400 times greater","About 40 times greater"],"answer":2,"exp":"The glomerular capillary membrane's Kf is roughly 400 times greater than in many other tissues, letting it filter several hundred times more water and solutes than a typical capillary membrane. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Albumin has a molecular diameter of about 6 nanometers, while glomerular endothelial pores are thought to be about 8 nanometers. Given that albumin is smaller than the pores, why is it still not normally filtered?","choices":["Albumin's negative charge causes electrostatic repulsion by the negatively charged barrier","Albumin is simply too large to pass, regardless of the stated pore size estimate","Albumin is actively pumped back into the capillary after filtration","Albumin binds irreversibly to podocyte foot processes before it can be filtered"],"answer":0,"exp":"Even though albumin (6 nm) is technically smaller than the estimated pore size (8 nm), its negative charge is electrostatically repelled by the negative charges of the filtration barrier, largely preventing its filtration. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which best summarizes the composition of normal glomerular filtrate?","choices":["Essentially free of cells and containing only tiny amounts of protein, but including salts and organic molecules like glucose","Rich in blood cells but completely free of protein, salts, and organic molecules like glucose.","Composed almost entirely of plasma protein, with no salts or organic molecules present at all.","Rich in both cellular elements and protein content, similar in composition to whole blood plasma."],"answer":0,"exp":"Normal glomerular filtrate has no cellular elements and only tiny amounts of protein, but does contain salts and organic molecules such as glucose. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"The high rate of glomerular filtration depends on which two general factors?","choices":["Only the special membrane properties, independent of blood flow","Only a high rate of blood flow, independent of any membrane properties at all","A high rate of blood flow plus special properties of the glomerular capillary membranes","A low rate of blood flow combined with a low glomerular Kf value"],"answer":2,"exp":"The high rate of glomerular filtration depends on both a high rate of blood flow and the special properties of the glomerular capillary membranes. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"GFR is determined by which two broad categories of factors, per this lecture's overview?","choices":["Physical forces and physiological regulatory mechanisms","Genetic predisposition and dietary sodium intake, exclusively","Patient age and biological sex differences, exclusively","Circadian time of day and hydration status, exclusively"],"answer":0,"exp":"GFR is determined by physical forces and by the physiological mechanisms that regulate GFR and renal blood flow. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which clinical-adjacent but still physiology-based reason is given for why GFR matters to assess?","choices":["It is unrelated to overall kidney function and instead reflects only systemic vascular resistance","It has no practical significance and is purely an academic calculation with no clinical bearing","It reflects how well the kidneys are filtering blood and whether kidney function is normal, decreased, or severely impaired","It only matters for calculating cardiac output and has no relevance to assessing renal function"],"answer":2,"exp":"GFR tells us how well the kidneys are filtering blood and whether kidney function is normal, decreased, or severely impaired, and helps monitor kidney function over time. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"All layers of the glomerular capillary wall together act as a barrier primarily to:","choices":["Water molecules specifically, while blocking the passage of essentially all solutes and proteins","Small solutes specifically, while completely blocking the passage of water molecules themselves","Plasma proteins, while still permitting rapid filtration of water and most solutes","Red blood cells specifically, while placing no restriction at all on plasma proteins"],"answer":2,"exp":"All layers of the glomerular capillary wall provide a barrier to the filtration of plasma proteins while still permitting rapid filtration of water and most solutes. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Under normal conditions, glomerular hydrostatic pressure (PG) is approximately:","choices":["10 mmHg","60 mmHg","120 mmHg","200 mmHg"],"answer":1,"exp":"Under normal conditions, glomerular hydrostatic pressure is approximately 60 mmHg. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"According to the lecture, changes in which pressure are the primary means of physiological regulation of GFR?","choices":["Central venous pressure","Glomerular hydrostatic pressure (PG)","Bowman's capsule hydrostatic pressure (PB)","Glomerular colloid osmotic pressure (πG) alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Changes in glomerular hydrostatic pressure (PG) are the primary means of physiologic regulation of GFR — an increase raises GFR, a decrease reduces it. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which three variables determine glomerular hydrostatic pressure (PG), per this lecture?","choices":["Plasma glucose concentration, plasma sodium concentration, and plasma potassium concentration","Respiratory rate, tidal volume, and total alveolar minute ventilation","Cardiac output, hematocrit percentage, and core body temperature reading","Arterial pressure, afferent arteriolar resistance, and efferent arteriolar resistance"],"answer":3,"exp":"Arterial pressure, afferent arteriolar resistance, and efferent arteriolar resistance are the three variables that determine glomerular hydrostatic pressure. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What is the effect of mild-to-moderate efferent arteriolar constriction on glomerular hydrostatic pressure and GFR?","choices":["Glomerular hydrostatic pressure decreases while GFR increases","Both glomerular hydrostatic pressure and GFR decrease substantially","Neither glomerular hydrostatic pressure nor GFR changes appreciably","Glomerular hydrostatic pressure increases and GFR increases slightly"],"answer":3,"exp":"Mild-to-moderate efferent arteriolar constriction increases glomerular hydrostatic pressure, which increases GFR slightly. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"What happens if efferent arterioles constrict too severely?","choices":["Renal blood flow decreases while filtration fraction and glomerular colloid osmotic pressure increase","Renal blood flow, filtration fraction, and glomerular colloid osmotic pressure all decrease","Renal blood flow increases sharply while filtration fraction holds steady","GFR increases indefinitely as efferent resistance continues rising unchecked"],"answer":0,"exp":"Severe efferent arteriolar constriction decreases renal blood flow, while filtration fraction and glomerular colloid osmotic pressure both increase as the efferent arteriole over-constricts. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which hormonal factors are listed as regulators of GFR in this lecture?","choices":["Insulin and glucagon","Angiotensin II/RAAS and ANP","Thyroid hormone and calcitonin","Growth hormone and prolactin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Angiotensin II/RAAS and atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP) are listed as hormonal regulators of GFR, alongside neural regulation by the sympathetic nervous system and renal prostaglandins. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Approximately what percentage of resting cardiac output do the kidneys receive?","choices":["About 75%","About 50%","About 5%","About 22%"],"answer":3,"exp":"The kidneys receive approximately 22% of cardiac output, though this is highly variable with body size, age, and sex. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 57"}]};
